# System Design

## What I am learning

How to reason about systems at larger scale: how to turn requirements into a design, which building blocks to use, and what each choice costs in latency, availability, consistency, and complexity.

## Why it matters

Architecture decides how code is organized inside a service. System design decides how services, data stores, caches, and queues fit together under load and failure. It is also how I will explain and defend technical decisions, in my own project and in interviews.

> **Goal:** be able to take requirements and design a system while explaining its trade-offs.

---

## Learning objectives

By the end of this topic I can:

- Turn vague requirements into functional and non-functional requirements
- Estimate load (requests per second, storage, bandwidth) with back-of-the-envelope math
- Explain scalability, availability, reliability, latency, and throughput, and how they differ
- Explain vertical versus horizontal scaling
- Place load balancers, caches, queues, and replicas in a design and justify each
- Explain replication, partitioning, and consistency trade-offs, including CAP-related trade-offs
- Explain fault tolerance and design for failure
- Make operations safe to repeat with idempotency
- Apply rate limiting, retries with backoff, timeouts, and circuit breakers
- Present a design clearly, with diagrams and explicit trade-offs

---

## Weekly progression

| Week | Focus |
|---|---|
| 1 | Requirements, estimation, and the core vocabulary (latency, throughput, availability) |
| 2 | Scaling: load balancing, stateless services, caching layers |
| 3 | Data at scale: replication, partitioning, consistency, CAP-related trade-offs |
| 4 | Asynchronous design: queues, workers, delivery guarantees, idempotency |
| 5 | Resilience: timeouts, retries, backoff, rate limiting, circuit breakers |
| 6 | Case studies, first half: design systems end to end |
| 7 | Case studies, second half and apply the lessons to the main project |
| 8 | Review and timed practice without notes |

This topic runs alongside Architecture. It prepares the distributed stage of the main project (Stage 6), but nothing is split until a concrete reason exists.

---

## Checklist

- [ ] Separate functional from non-functional requirements
- [ ] Do a back-of-the-envelope estimate (traffic, storage, bandwidth)
- [ ] Explain latency versus throughput
- [ ] Explain availability and how it is measured (nines)
- [ ] Explain vertical versus horizontal scaling
- [ ] Explain why stateless services scale more easily
- [ ] Explain load balancing strategies (round robin, least connections, hashing)
- [ ] Choose and justify a caching strategy (aside, write-through, write-back) and its invalidation
- [ ] Explain replication (leader/follower) and replication lag
- [ ] Explain partitioning and how to choose a partition key
- [ ] Explain consistency models and CAP-related trade-offs
- [ ] Explain message queues and delivery guarantees (at-most, at-least, exactly-once)
- [ ] Design an idempotent operation
- [ ] Apply timeouts, retries with backoff, and jitter
- [ ] Explain rate limiting algorithms (token bucket, sliding window)
- [ ] Explain the circuit breaker pattern
- [ ] Identify single points of failure
- [ ] Present a design and defend its trade-offs

A box is ticked only when I can do or explain the task without relying entirely on a tutorial.

---

## A repeatable design method

Use the same steps for every design problem:

```text
1. Clarify requirements          what must it do, and for whom?
2. Define non-functional needs   scale, latency, availability, consistency
3. Estimate load                 requests, data size, growth
4. Sketch the simplest design    start with one service and one database
5. Find the bottleneck           what fails first as load grows?
6. Add components deliberately   cache, queue, replica, partition, each with a reason
7. Plan for failure              what happens when each part is down or slow?
8. State the trade-offs          what did this design give up?
```

Always start with the simplest design that meets the requirements and add complexity only when a requirement demands it.

---

## Exercises

- [ ] Estimate daily traffic, storage, and peak requests per second for three products
- [ ] Compute the availability of two services in series versus in parallel
- [ ] Implement a token-bucket rate limiter and test it
- [ ] Implement retries with exponential backoff and jitter, and show why plain retries can make an outage worse
- [ ] Make a payment-style endpoint idempotent with an idempotency key
- [ ] Choose a partition key for an orders table and show one query that becomes expensive
- [ ] Draw the request path of a read through a load balancer, cache, and database

## Labs

Each lab ends with a written note: symptom, evidence, hypothesis, root cause, fix, lesson.

- [ ] **Cache stampede:** many requests hit the database when a hot key expires, reproduce and mitigate it
- [ ] **Retry storm:** a failing dependency is overloaded by retries, add backoff and a circuit breaker
- [ ] **Duplicate processing:** a queue delivers a message twice, make the consumer idempotent
- [ ] **Replica lag:** a user writes and then reads stale data, explain it and choose a fix
- [ ] **Load test:** run a load test on the backend, find the first bottleneck, and fix it with evidence

## Case studies

Written designs stored in `case-studies/`. Each follows the design method above and ends with a trade-off summary.

- [ ] URL shortener
- [ ] Rate limiter service
- [ ] Notification system (email, push, retries)
- [ ] Order processing for the e-commerce project (inventory, payment, idempotency)
- [ ] News feed or chat system (read-heavy versus write-heavy)

